---
description: Always apply Pyramid Principle structure to keep exported-first layouts.
alwaysApply: true
---

Apply the Pyramid Principle in every file:

- Always put exported APIs (functions, constants, components, and default exports) before internal implementation details.
- Make the conclusion/entry point visible first, then place the supporting logic and internal types below it.
- Keep supporting blocks in logical groups (2nd-level points), then detail subpoints only as needed.
- Keep internals ordered by necessity so readers can scan top-down from intent to mechanism.
- Keep type declarations and helpers as focused as possible; when practical, use interfaces instead of object type aliases.

## Ordering details

- Types can be defined right before the function that uses them, or immediately
  after the first type/interface that reference them.
- Internal helper functions should be defined after the exported function that calls them.
- For type-only files (no runtime functions): order by dependency chain.
  Foundational shared types (like `InputPaths`) first, then base interfaces,
  then primary public interfaces that extend them, then supporting enums and
  leaf types, then peripheral wrapper aliases.
